{"v": 1, "ausgabe": 8, "datum": "2026-11-21", "titel": "", "erstellt": "2026-11-21T17:53:24+01:00", "abgeglichen": "2026-11-21T17:53:24+01:00", "wetter": "Salzburg 3 to 7 °C, rain showers, near-certain rain", "sonne": "Sunrise 7:22", "kurz": ["Consulting Deep Dive, part 6: Oliver Wyman and Kearney, the specialists who win where MBB is merely good", "The IPO pop lasted a day: Anthropic slides towards its offering price as banks exercise the greenshoe", "A payout every week: what Europe's first weekly-distributing world ETF really delivers"], "anzahlMeldungen": 4, "ressorts": [{"name": "Consulting", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Consulting Deep Dive, part 6: Oliver Wyman and Kearney, the specialists who win where MBB is merely good", "text": "Below McKinsey, BCG and Bain sits a tier of firms whose brand is narrower but, in their home turf, every bit as strong. Two of them are worth a close look for you: Oliver Wyman, the house of financial services, and Kearney, the house of operations and procurement. Choosing either is less a step down than a bet on a specialism, and that bet shapes your exits for a decade.\n\nOliver Wyman belongs to Marsh McLennan, the insurance broking and advisory group that also owns Mercer and NERA Economic Consulting. Headcount estimates range from roughly 5,500 to about 7,000 depending on the source, spread across some 70 cities in 30 countries. Its flagship is financial services: strategy, risk, regulation and transformation work for banks, insurers and asset managers. Only this week it agreed to buy Synpulse, a Swiss consultancy specialising in technology for financial institutions, to deepen its AI practice, which tells you where the firm sees its next decade.\n\nThat focus defines the exits. Career guides and forum accounts consistently describe financial services as the widest funnel: senior strategy, risk and corporate development roles at banks, insurers and asset managers, then corporate strategy at large companies and value creation teams at private equity funds. One guide puts it pointedly: Oliver Wyman opens doors in financial services that most consultancies, MBB included, cannot, but demands more deliberate effort than MBB for generalist exits such as large-cap private equity. Private equity is achievable, particularly from the financial services practice, but it is not the default path.\n\nKearney, spun out of the old A.T. Kearney, is built around operations. Its own site says procurement \"isn't a side offering. It's our core\", and Management Consulted describes its strongest reputation as operations, procurement and supply chain transformation. Juniors start in a general practice across industries and functions and typically choose a practice only at senior associate or manager level, so you are not locked in on day one. Current postings show where it is heading: its Digital and Analytics practice now recruits for \"agentic procurement transformation\", AI agents running purchasing processes.\n\nKearney's typical exits follow from that profile: corporate strategy and transformation roles, procurement and cost leadership, private equity portfolio operations (the people who fix companies after the deal, rather than those who pick them), and graduate programmes. One coaching site sums up the trade-off: Kearney trails MBB on brand pull and access to private equity deal teams, matches it in corporate strategy at manager and director level, and beats it in operations, supply chain and procurement leadership. A word of caution: none of the sources offers hard placement statistics, and most come from interview-prep firms, so treat the patterns as directional.\n\nThe long-term arithmetic is simple. Oliver Wyman is the natural springboard if you can picture yourself as a bank's head of strategy, a chief risk officer or an investor in fintech; Kearney if you see yourself as COO, head of procurement or an operating partner at a buyout fund. Neither is ideal if your single goal is a large-cap private equity deal team; there, MBB still has the edge.", "url": "https://www.hackingthecaseinterview.com/pages/oliver-wyman-exit-opportunities", "quelle": "Hacking the Case Interview: Oliver Wyman exits", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@ef1eff4f566ca3f93b684bb86dcecd9cff72d175/f3aa2c0c829cac1a.mp3"}]}, {"name": "AI and Claude", "meldungen": [{"titel": "The IPO pop lasted a day: Anthropic slides towards its offering price as banks exercise the greenshoe", "text": "Anthropic's debut euphoria proved short-lived. On its second day as a listed company the stock surrendered most of its first-day gains: almost an hour after the opening bell it stood 11.4 per cent lower at 86.11 dollars, the Financial Times reported, barely 4 per cent above the 83-dollar offering price. heise saw it trading at around 85 dollars later in the day. On Thursday the shares had opened at 111.50 dollars, touched 115.10 and closed at 97.17. The fully diluted valuation has thus shrunk from 1.75 trillion dollars at Thursday's close to about 1.56 trillion.\n\nAt the same time the underwriters exercised the greenshoe, an option to sell up to 15 per cent more shares than originally offered. Anthropic had granted the banks 147 million extra shares, which at 83 dollars brings in about 12.2 billion dollars and lifts the IPO proceeds to roughly 93.5 billion. Including a 26-billion-dollar private placement, the company has raised 119.5 billion dollars. \"We think that positions us really well,\" the chief financial officer, Krishna Rao, told Bloomberg. The money is needed: Anthropic posted an operating loss of 567 million dollars in the third quarter, partly because of 5 billion dollars in computing costs. The catch for shareholders is that exercising the option removes the banks' built-in price cushion.\n\nThe slide is part of a wider sell-off. Investors fret about debt-funded AI spending, the troubles at Nvidia and the Democratic gains in the midterms, which could make new data centres harder to approve. Chris Beauchamp of IG also sees a self-inflicted effect: investors are selling other stocks to pay for their new Anthropic shares. Demand had been enormous, with the book reportedly at least six times oversubscribed.\n\nHistory counsels patience rather than panic: Facebook and Uber each lost 11 per cent on their second day, though both then fell further. Cathie Wood's ARK bought shares worth 90.8 million dollars after the debut. The next milestones are a possible Nasdaq-100 inclusion as early as December, quarterly figures in February and, in about 180 days, the end of the insider lock-up.", "url": "https://www.trendingtopics.eu/anthropic-shares-premarket-greenshoe/", "quelle": "Trending Topics", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@ef1eff4f566ca3f93b684bb86dcecd9cff72d175/f6b60c01b79cd20d.mp3"}]}, {"name": "Personal Finance", "meldungen": [{"titel": "A payout every week: what Europe's first weekly-distributing world ETF really delivers", "text": "Dividend ETFs that pay monthly are old news; now Europe has one that promises to pay every week. The HANetf Weekly World Equity Income UCITS ETF (ISIN IE000I50V6C7) has been tradable on Xetra since 16 September 2026 and is billed as the first ETF in Europe to distribute weekly, with 52 payment dates a year.\n\nThe headline figure of around 8 per cent deserves scrutiny. As Finanzwissen points out, it is an indicative projection, not a track record of actual distributions. The payouts so far have been small: 0.0191 dollars per share for the first one, 0.0152 for the most recent, according to one report. Distributions also fluctuate, so the weekly rhythm says nothing about the size or safety of the income.\n\nThe broader lesson applies to all distribution products: the frequency of payments changes your cash flow, not your return. What matters is total return after costs and taxes, and every payout counts as taxable income in the year it is received.", "url": "https://finanzwissen.de/news/erster-wochen-ausschuetter-europas-hanetf-startet-welt-etf-mit-52-zahltagen-im-jahr/", "quelle": "Finanzwissen", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@ef1eff4f566ca3f93b684bb86dcecd9cff72d175/be33c01efe2aec7b.mp3"}]}, {"name": "Start-ups and Venture Capital", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Austria's long-promised fund of funds finally gets a name, a budget and a search for a manager", "text": "After years of lobbying by the start-up scene, Austria's government has put its fund of funds for start-ups and scale-ups on track. The state secretary for the economy, Elisabeth Zehetner (ÖVP), and the finance minister, Markus Marterbauer (SPÖ), announced that the vehicle will be called the Austrian Venture & Growth Fund of Funds and is meant to reach a volume of 500 million euros.\n\nThe federal government will commit up to 100 million euros as an anchor investment over a multi-year investment period. The design rests on leverage: for every public euro, four euros are supposed to come from private and institutional investors. According to the government, the state's share comes from returns on existing equity programmes, so it does not burden the budget.\n\nThe next step is finding someone to run it. Austria Wirtschaftsservice (aws, the federal promotional bank) manages the federal stake and will open the tender as early as next week. A panel of experts will be involved; the manager is to have international experience and run the fund independently on commercial criteria. The choice is planned for early 2027, after which the fund should become operational, with a lifetime of roughly 16 years.\n\nCrucially, the fund will not back companies directly. It invests in venture capital and private equity funds, which in turn finance start-ups, scale-ups and spin-offs. The aim is to give insurers, banks, pension funds and foundations an easier route into growth capital as an asset class. The focus lies on the key technologies of the national industrial strategy; the fund may invest abroad but should above all ease access to capital for Austrian firms. Success will be measured, among other things, by how much Austrian investor money it attracts and how much flows into domestic companies.\n\nThe backdrop is sobering. The industrial strategy aims to triple venture capital as a share of GDP by 2035, yet for larger rounds Austrian start-ups depend almost entirely on foreign investors, and according to the Atomico report Austria invests less in start-ups relative to its economy than almost any other major European tech nation. Zehetner cited Germany and Denmark as proof that public anchor money can mobilise private risk capital; Marterbauer stressed that the public funds are expected to flow back over time.\n\nThe real test is whether the targeted 400 million euros of private capital materialise. Large domestic institutions such as Erste Group and Uniqa already invest in funds of funds in Poland and at EU level, but still had open questions about the Austrian model.", "url": "https://www.trendingtopics.eu/startup-dachfonds-fondsmanagement-gesucht/", "quelle": "Trending Topics", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@ef1eff4f566ca3f93b684bb86dcecd9cff72d175/1f1c2964c1960b0d.mp3"}]}], "fuss": "Created on 21 November 2026 at 07:40 by Claude. Sources linked under each report. No verifiable full texts this morning for Work and Pay, Health and Sleep or Strategy (the AK Niederösterreich wage case, the WELT sleep piece and a McDonald's strategy hire could not be confirmed). Gmail could not be checked; the connector needs a fresh sign-in. Last updated 17:53.", "privat": {"iv": "lxfeJBfkauYxbrpE0YmIlg==", "ct": "", "mac": "FgT7uTTeu9pjWav58TdFC9QOr1m2oR+JM7W2vKx6UN4="}, "audioGesamt": {"url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@ef1eff4f566ca3f93b684bb86dcecd9cff72d175/g1e3313e68d6d7ea.mp3", "kapitel": [0.0, 225.96, 390.024, 471.528], "dauer": 646.824}}